<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mountstuart Elphinstone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mountstuart Elphinstone was a Scottish administrator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. As resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> he played a leading part in the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-maratha-war">Third Anglo-Maratha War</a> of 1817–1818 and then organised the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> territories. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> from 1819 to 1827 he promoted education and a legal code, and he later wrote a well-known History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mountstuart-elphinstone.jpg" alt="Mountstuart Elphinstone" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountstuart_Elphinstone_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mountstuart Elphinstone was born in Dumbarton on 6 October 1779 and educated at the Royal High School. He was the fourth son of the 11th Baron Elphinstone. Appointed to the civil service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, one of whose directors was his uncle, he arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> early in 1796. In 1799 he escaped a massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> by followers of the deposed Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, Wazir Ali Khan. In 1801 he moved to the diplomatic service as assistant to the British resident Josiah Webbe at the court of the Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elphinstone was attached to the mission of Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and, when war broke out, acted as virtual aide-de-camp to Wellesley, showing at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-assaye">Battle of Assaye</a> such courage that Wellesley told him he ought to have been a soldier. In 1804 he became British resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>. In 1808 he was appointed the first British envoy to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, to secure an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s planned advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The alliance proved of little value because Shah Shuja was driven from the throne, but Elphinstone's Account of the Kingdom of Cabul, of 1815, was the lasting result.</p><p>In 1811 he became resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> declared war on the British in 1817 he assumed command at the Battle of Khadki despite his non-military background and secured a victory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s territories were annexed, and he became Commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in 1818. From 1819 to 1827 he served at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, where he promoted education, was credited with the Elphinstone code of criminal law, and returned many lands to the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1829, Elphinstone was one of the seven founders of the Royal Geographical Society on 16 July 1830. He twice refused appointment as Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in order to finish his two-volume History of India, published in 1841. He died in Hookwood, Surrey, on 20 November 1859 and was buried in Limpsfield churchyard. Elphinstone College was endowed in his memory, and there is a memorial to him in the crypt of St Paul's Cathedral. James Sutherland Cotton wrote his biography in 1892.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mountstuart Elphinstone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mountstuart Elphinstone</b> organised the Deccan conquests.</blockquote><a href="/state/bombay-presidency">Bombay Presidency</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dumbarton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Woking, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-4.55&amp;year=1831" aria-label="Dumbarton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mountstuart Elphinstone"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4782 5339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4782 5339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-4.55&amp;year=1831">What was here in 1831? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
